00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.786
- [Voiceover] Brendan
asks, "Does VaynerMedia

00:01:03.786 --> 00:01:06.460
"focus much on winning
awards, and what's your take

00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:09.359
"on the ad industry's obsession
with awards in general?"

00:01:10.472 --> 00:01:11.643
- Brendan, great question.

00:01:11.643 --> 00:01:14.902
One that I've been talking
about for a long time

00:01:14.902 --> 00:01:17.187
in these halls and in the industry.

00:01:17.187 --> 00:01:19.919
I think awards are horsecrap.

00:01:19.919 --> 00:01:22.620
I don't even know why I'm
not saying horse (beep),

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:26.076
because the reason
agencies want to win them

00:01:26.076 --> 00:01:27.330
is for two reasons.

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:29.087
It allows them to recruit talent

00:01:29.087 --> 00:01:32.595
and they use it as something
to get more business

00:01:32.595 --> 00:01:34.741
and what they're doing is,
they're putting out work

00:01:34.741 --> 00:01:37.362
for clients that is actually
trying to get awards

00:01:37.362 --> 00:01:40.007
versus actually trying to sell something,

00:01:40.007 --> 00:01:41.595
and that's my real problem with it.

00:01:41.595 --> 00:01:44.576
I don't begrudge the
agencies that focus on it

00:01:44.576 --> 00:01:46.529
because I understand
their business rationale

00:01:46.529 --> 00:01:49.174
but for me, it's a unhealthy culture

00:01:49.174 --> 00:01:51.431
because it takes your eye off the prize

00:01:51.431 --> 00:01:53.297
which is actually do
something for business.

00:01:53.297 --> 00:01:56.138
And for us, you know, we're
lucky because of our work

00:01:56.138 --> 00:01:58.034
and because of my personal
brand, to be honest with you,

00:01:58.034 --> 00:01:59.820
we've been able to get
a lot of new business.

00:01:59.820 --> 00:02:02.354
And now, our work is the
word-of-mouth of our business

00:02:02.354 --> 00:02:04.807
and two, as you can tell, people know

00:02:04.807 --> 00:02:07.341
this is the right place to
work, so we don't focus on it.

00:02:07.341 --> 00:02:09.765
I understand why people do focus on it.

00:02:09.765 --> 00:02:12.190
I think it's an energy sucker away from

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:15.170
the thing that matters, which
is you need to sell something.

00:02:15.977 --> 00:02:18.012
- [Voiceover] Rich asks,
"What advice do you have

00:02:18.012 --> 00:02:20.163
"for professors to engage college students

00:02:20.163 --> 00:02:22.582
"in the classroom? What
about outside of class?"

00:02:23.893 --> 00:02:26.432
- Rich, I think the biggest
thing for you and any

00:02:26.432 --> 00:02:28.213
professor out there, or if
you're asking for somebody

00:02:28.213 --> 00:02:31.750
is that professors aren't relevant

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.399
to a lot of their students
today, more than ever,

00:02:34.399 --> 00:02:37.323
because the behaviors of
how we act aren't mapping,

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:40.000
meaning so many people have emailed me

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:43.035
like, literally because of
this show, emailed me that,

00:02:43.035 --> 00:02:45.904
"Hey I'm in class right
now, and what my professor's

00:02:45.904 --> 00:02:47.829
"talking about makes
no sense, compared to.

00:02:47.829 --> 00:02:51.198
"I'm listening to your podcast right now

00:02:51.198 --> 00:02:54.347
"while my professor's talking
because he or she are so."

00:02:54.347 --> 00:02:56.719
And literally professors
in major universities

00:02:56.719 --> 00:02:58.725
that are putting kids in debt right now

00:02:58.725 --> 00:03:01.898
are telling them that there
is no ROI in social media,

00:03:01.898 --> 00:03:05.411
like ludicrous horsecrap,
I'm on this crap kick.

00:03:05.411 --> 00:03:08.641
Anyway, I think the biggest
thing a professor can do

00:03:08.641 --> 00:03:10.509
is to be relevant with them.

00:03:10.509 --> 00:03:13.297
If you're a professor right
now, and especially if you're a

00:03:13.297 --> 00:03:15.610
marketing or comms professor right now,

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:18.533
and you're not jamming
on YikYak and Snapchat

00:03:18.533 --> 00:03:21.461
and the Insta, then you're
making a huge mistake

00:03:21.461 --> 00:03:23.827
because you're out of
touch with your students.

00:03:23.827 --> 00:03:26.779
You're saying dumb shit like,
"Hey kids, hey students,

00:03:26.779 --> 00:03:29.650
"we're gonna connect
through modern things like."

00:03:30.154 --> 00:03:33.802
It can't even come out of
my mouth, I'm so disgusted

00:03:33.802 --> 00:03:36.922
with what's going on in
universities across this country

00:03:36.922 --> 00:03:39.989
and so it's relevance,
there is eye rolling

00:03:39.989 --> 00:03:42.633
and checking the (beep)
out going on in classrooms

00:03:42.633 --> 00:03:44.615
all across this country and it's sad

00:03:44.615 --> 00:03:48.485
because of the romance of how
professors think it should be

00:03:48.485 --> 00:03:51.384
versus the reality of what it's like.

00:03:53.221 --> 00:03:55.007
- [Voiceover] Tanner
asks, "What are your tips

00:03:55.007 --> 00:03:56.903
"for teenage bloggers to show brands

00:03:56.903 --> 00:03:58.767
"that they mean business?"

00:03:59.965 --> 00:04:03.451
- Tanner, brands don't care
if you're 14 or 41 or 4,000.

00:04:03.451 --> 00:04:07.041
By having actual results,
they'll think you're in business.

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:09.302
So I don't know, have
traffic, have followers,

00:04:09.302 --> 00:04:11.864
show them engagement, that's business,

00:04:11.864 --> 00:04:13.728
show them your results, son.

00:04:14.648 --> 00:04:17.935
- [Voiceover] Tanova asks,
"How do you deal with burnout?"

00:04:18.579 --> 00:04:19.861
- Tanova, this is a great question.

00:04:19.861 --> 00:04:20.974
I personally selected this one.

00:04:20.974 --> 00:04:23.677
I saw it in my Twitter
stream and sent it to India.

00:04:23.873 --> 00:04:25.602
Show India, I like when we do that.

00:04:25.602 --> 00:04:26.158
- Eh.

00:04:26.158 --> 00:04:27.579
- That's my favorite part of the show.

00:04:29.339 --> 00:04:32.064
You know, it's really funny,
this is a funny question.

00:04:32.064 --> 00:04:35.535
I burn out once every

00:04:36.985 --> 00:04:38.463
six or seven years,

00:04:38.463 --> 00:04:41.530
I hit a real like ugh
spot, like where I wanna

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:44.175
just check out and I go to sleep.

00:04:44.482 --> 00:04:45.960
I actually go home and go to sleep.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.106
It hasn't actually
happened, actually I'm on a

00:04:48.106 --> 00:04:49.747
real good run right now, I
think the last time I did

00:04:49.747 --> 00:04:52.675
was when we lost Texas at Wine Library,

00:04:52.675 --> 00:04:53.928
and couldn't ship there anymore

00:04:53.928 --> 00:04:54.984
and we lost like four million in revenue

00:04:54.984 --> 00:04:57.019
and I was just burnt out
like fighting the fight

00:04:57.019 --> 00:04:59.582
of like in that world,
and so I just literally

00:04:59.582 --> 00:05:01.756
went home at like 6 PM and went to sleep.

00:05:01.756 --> 00:05:04.099
I haven't done it since then,
and that was like 2002 or 3,

00:05:04.099 --> 00:05:04.953
so it's been a little, maybe it's not even

00:05:04.953 --> 00:05:07.348
six or seven years but,
when I hit my lowest point,

00:05:07.348 --> 00:05:09.493
I do two things, I go to sleep immediately

00:05:09.493 --> 00:05:12.278
and two, I make pretend
that my mom was killed.

00:05:12.979 --> 00:05:14.730
And I know that's an intense statement,

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:16.991
and you should have just
the collective reaction,

00:05:16.991 --> 00:05:19.218
but when I burn out from work.

00:05:19.218 --> 00:05:20.164
(laughs)

00:05:20.164 --> 00:05:20.975
It's intense.

00:05:22.985 --> 00:05:25.285
When I burn out, it means that I'm hurt

00:05:25.285 --> 00:05:27.072
by whatever's going on in business

00:05:27.072 --> 00:05:30.082
and I'm focusing on business
instead of the big picture

00:05:30.082 --> 00:05:32.285
and I directly put my
brain into a place of

00:05:32.285 --> 00:05:34.512
what do I really care
about, and the second

00:05:34.512 --> 00:05:37.334
I do that extreme move,
I'm already in the process

00:05:37.334 --> 00:05:39.702
of going back upstream and so look,

00:05:39.702 --> 00:05:42.605
I'm a positive person, I
put things in perspective

00:05:42.605 --> 00:05:45.110
in a very healthy way, I think and so

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:47.620
I don't tend to burnout that often,

00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.184
but the couple times I've hit rock bottom,

00:05:50.184 --> 00:05:53.020
it's been sleep and recalibration.

00:05:53.774 --> 00:05:57.630
- Alright, here's my real question.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:06:01.074
Which social media platform
that is currently an incumbent

00:06:01.074 --> 00:06:04.947
do you feel will be extinct in five years.

00:06:04.947 --> 00:06:08.961
In 2020, which social media platform

00:06:08.961 --> 00:06:11.024
that everybody's invested in

00:06:11.024 --> 00:06:13.559
do you think will be extinct?

00:06:16.319 --> 00:06:19.241
- Thanks for the question, Ashton.

00:06:19.717 --> 00:06:23.423
So I think the two most
vulnerable platforms

00:06:23.423 --> 00:06:27.574
are Tumblr and Google Plus,
and I think that because

00:06:27.574 --> 00:06:29.859
they're just owned by
big conglomerates, right.

00:06:29.859 --> 00:06:32.253
You know, for all my concerns
about where Twitter's going,

00:06:32.253 --> 00:06:35.176
I feel like Dick Costolo and
team will figure that out

00:06:35.176 --> 00:06:36.375
and get better.

00:06:36.375 --> 00:06:40.000
Facebook, Instagram, Snapchat
are really in a great spot.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.785
The two incumbents, Pinterest
I think is in a great spot.

00:06:42.785 --> 00:06:44.233
About to sneeze possibly, by the way,

00:06:44.233 --> 00:06:45.485
as I answer this question.

00:06:45.485 --> 00:06:48.135
The two incumbents that
I'm most worried about

00:06:48.135 --> 00:06:50.763
are Google Plus and Tumblr.

00:06:50.763 --> 00:06:52.426
Tumblr because it's owned by Yahoo

00:06:52.426 --> 00:06:56.160
and all those politics
of being independent

00:06:56.160 --> 00:06:58.330
but still being within
a big holding company,

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:00.173
and Google Plus because Google,

00:07:00.173 --> 00:07:01.675
this is a positive by the way.

00:07:01.675 --> 00:07:04.546
Google has shown me the enormous ability

00:07:04.546 --> 00:07:07.248
to cut bait if something's not working,

00:07:07.248 --> 00:07:08.837
no matter how big it was.

00:07:08.837 --> 00:07:10.537
And I just have a funny feeling

00:07:10.537 --> 00:07:13.296
that they may just come out
in a year or two or three

00:07:13.296 --> 00:07:14.601
and just say, "We're cutting it.

00:07:14.601 --> 00:07:16.526
"We may get back into
this game, we may not

00:07:16.526 --> 00:07:19.089
"but this execution is not working for us."

00:07:19.089 --> 00:07:23.351
I actually am almost willing
to predict that may happen

00:07:23.351 --> 00:07:26.889
that I can see them
cutting Google Plus out,

00:07:26.889 --> 00:07:29.927
completely outright in the next 36 months.

00:07:29.927 --> 00:07:32.323
So those are two incumbents, Ashton,

00:07:32.323 --> 00:07:34.276
and I appreciate the question.

